# question: CQ-0017
# answers: If section 43 were amended, which parts of the Manual would need reviewing?
# limits: Reviewing effort, not reviewing outcome. A Part appears because a passage in it cites the provision, not because an amendment would change what that passage means. The `inferred` column matters: those citations are upstream reading a bare "section 43" out of prose, not a link IP Australia wrote, so a Part carried only by inferred edges is a weaker claim than its count suggests. **And it counts the passages the graph holds, which is not every passage in the corpus that cites the provision.** The graph holds what this repository has said something about (ADR-0097), so for section 43 this returns 56 of the corpus's 67 citing passages; the 11 missing ones are passages no record in either store speaks about. `tmk-recon --provision TMA1995/s43` counts all 67 off the loader and is the number to quote for corpus coverage. Before the section 43 boundary was withdrawn this query was complete for section 43 and incomplete for every other provision, because the fence was drawn around that one; it is now uniformly incomplete and says so.

PREFIX tmk:  <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/>
PREFIX tmkg: <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/>

SELECT ?part (COUNT(DISTINCT ?chunk) AS ?passages)
       (SUM(IF(?extraction = "href", 1, 0)) AS ?authored)
       (SUM(IF(BOUND(?certainty) && ?certainty = "default", 1, 0)) AS ?inferred)
WHERE {
  GRAPH tmkg:source {
    ?citation a tmk:Citation ;
              tmk:citingPassage ?chunk ;
              tmk:citedAuthority <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s43> ;
              tmk:extraction ?extraction .
    OPTIONAL { ?citation tmk:certainty ?certainty }
    ?chunk tmk:partId ?part .
  }
}
GROUP BY ?part
ORDER BY DESC(?passages) ?part
